Yes — in that revision it’s an `@apply` bug/limitation.

`borderStylesReset` deliberately emits an early:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; }
```

Then the generated stylesheet later emits the style rule. With classes, source order makes this work alongside `.border`:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; }
.border { border: 1px solid ...; }
.border-dotted { border-style: dotted; }
```

But `@apply` copies declarations inline, so `@apply border border-dotted` ends up with `border-width: 0` after the border shorthand and removes the visible border.

At that commit, use an explicit declaration after applying the border:

```css
.example {
  @apply border;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

So your reading of lines 61/64 is correct: the reset was designed for stylesheet cascade ordering and does not compose correctly through `@apply`.
